{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMfkAH9HKtjBFSZ4wvqQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012sq4b5eW8Vcie9g6NB9TN3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-grader-x6frbq3e/report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":223,"cache_read_input_tokens":6170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":223},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1dead49e-0ccc-40aa-98a0-e6dffc892fce","uuid":"5784a9fd-eee7-4eec-bdd4-e781929327d0","timestamp":"2026-10-24T20:09:52.090Z","request_id":"req_011CgMfkA21ugSw9L9BJGoni","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012sq4b5eW8Vcie9g6NB9TN3":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-grader-x6frbq3e/report.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012sq4b5eW8Vcie9g6NB9TN3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tI found three real problems in `service/routes.mjs`, plus a few smaller ones. I only read the code and didn't run anything.\n2\t\n3\t## Findings\n4\t\n5\t**1. An authenticated request can crash the process (high)**\n6\t- `sessions` is a plain `{}` (`routes.mjs:5`). The lookup `sessions[req.headers['mcp-session-id']]` (lines 17 and 22) also finds inherited keys.\n7\t- A header such as `mcp-session-id: constructor` or `toString` returns a truthy `Object.prototype` member, so the `!session` check passes. `session.handleRequest` is then `undefined`, and the call throws a `TypeError`.\n8\t- The handlers are `async`, and Express 4.22.2 doesn't catch rejected promises from them. The README says Node 20 runs with default unhandled-rejection behavior and the app has no hooks, so the process exits.\n9\t- Anyone holding the gateway key can do this. The same crash happens if the trusted `handleRequest` ever rejects, for example on a client abort or a malformed request.\n10\t- **Fix:** use a `Map`, or `Object.create(null)` plus `Object.hasOwn`. Wrap each handler body in `try/catch`, and send a 500 only if `!res.headersSent`. Also check that the session ID is a string.\n11\t\n12\t**2. Sessions are never removed (medium)**\n13\t- `DELETE /mcp` forwards to the transport but never deletes the entry from `sessions`. Nothing else expires entries either.\n14\t- `POST /mcp/init` has no cap or rate limit, so memory grows without bound. Each entry holds a transport object.\n15\t- **Fix:** on a successful DELETE, call `sessions.delete(id)`. Also add a transport close hook, an idle TTL, and a maximum session count.\n16\t\n17\t**3. There is no `POST /mcp` route (functional, medium)**\n18\t- Only `POST /mcp/init`, `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp` are registered. Client-to-server JSON-RPC messages have no route. If this is meant to be a Streamable HTTP MCP endpoint, it can't receive requests.\n19\t- No body parser is mounted either. That is fine if `handleRequest` reads the raw stream, but it should be confirmed.\n20\t- Either add the POST handler with the same session lookup, or document that the transport handles messages some other way.\n21\t\n22\t**4. The bearer check isn't constant-time (low)**\n23\t- `req.headers.authorization !== \\`Bearer ${secret}\\`` (line 7) is a plain string comparison, which leaks timing information in principle.\n24\t- With a single trusted principal and a high-entropy secret, the risk is small. It is still cheap to fix with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length hashes of both values.\n25\t\n26\t**5. Minor issues**\n27\t- The 401 response has no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n28\t- There is no failed-auth rate limiting. The README puts ingress policy under the host.\n29\t- The session lookup is copied into two handlers. Pull it into a helper so the fix for item 1 lives in one place.\n30\t- `GATEWAY_KEY` is read once at registration. That is fine, but rotating the key needs a restart.\n31\t\n32\t## Out of scope\n33\tThe README assigns these to the host, so I didn't assess them:\n34\t- `createTransport` internals\n35\t- the secret store\n36\t- audit logging\n37\t- framing limits\n38\t- process supervision\n39\t\n40\tSession IDs are random UUIDs and are only correlation handles behind the auth gate, so I don't count their guessability as an issue."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1dead49e-0ccc-40aa-98a0-e6dffc892fce","uuid":"6b7dbcf9-1d36-4adc-a68f-d073ee75b0a0","timestamp":"2026-10-24T20:09:52.128Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-grader-x6frbq3e/report.md","content":"I found three real problems in `service/routes.mjs`, plus a few smaller ones. I only read the code and didn't run anything.\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. An authenticated request can crash the process (high)**\n- `sessions` is a plain `{}` (`routes.mjs:5`). The lookup `sessions[req.headers['mcp-session-id']]` (lines 17 and 22) also finds inherited keys.\n- A header such as `mcp-session-id: constructor` or `toString` returns a truthy `Object.prototype` member, so the `!session` check passes. `session.handleRequest` is then `undefined`, and the call throws a `TypeError`.\n- The handlers are `async`, and Express 4.22.2 doesn't catch rejected promises from them. The README says Node 20 runs with default unhandled-rejection behavior and the app has no hooks, so the process exits.\n- Anyone holding the gateway key can do this. The same crash happens if the trusted `handleRequest` ever rejects, for example on a client abort or a malformed request.\n- **Fix:** use a `Map`, or `Object.create(null)` plus `Object.hasOwn`. Wrap each handler body in `try/catch`, and send a 500 only if `!res.headersSent`. Also check that the session ID is a string.\n\n**2. Sessions are never removed (medium)**\n- `DELETE /mcp` forwards to the transport but never deletes the entry from `sessions`. Nothing else expires entries either.\n- `POST /mcp/init` has no cap or rate limit, so memory grows without bound. Each entry holds a transport object.\n- **Fix:** on a successful DELETE, call `sessions.delete(id)`. Also add a transport close hook, an idle TTL, and a maximum session count.\n\n**3. There is no `POST /mcp` route (functional, medium)**\n- Only `POST /mcp/init`, `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp` are registered. Client-to-server JSON-RPC messages have no route. If this is meant to be a Streamable HTTP MCP endpoint, it can't receive requests.\n- No body parser is mounted either. That is fine if `handleRequest` reads the raw stream, but it should be confirmed.\n- Either add the POST handler with the same session lookup, or document that the transport handles messages some other way.\n\n**4. The bearer check isn't constant-time (low)**\n- `req.headers.authorization !== \\`Bearer ${secret}\\`` (line 7) is a plain string comparison, which leaks timing information in principle.\n- With a single trusted principal and a high-entropy secret, the risk is small. It is still cheap to fix with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length hashes of both values.\n\n**5. Minor issues**\n- The 401 response has no `WWW-Authenticate` header.\n- There is no failed-auth rate limiting. The README puts ingress policy under the host.\n- The session lookup is copied into two handlers. Pull it into a helper so the fix for item 1 lives in one place.\n- `GATEWAY_KEY` is read once at registration. That is fine, but rotating the key needs a restart.\n\n## Out of scope\nThe README assigns these to the host, so I didn't assess them:\n- `createTransport` internals\n- the secret store\n- audit logging\n- framing limits\n- process supervision\n\nSession IDs are random UUIDs and are only correlation handles behind the auth gate, so I don't count their guessability as an issue.","numLines":40,"startLine":1,"totalLines":40}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012sq4b5eW8Vcie9g6NB9TN3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
